  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 240 AM EST Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Above normal temperatures and tranquil weather \nthrough Saturday.\n\nHigh pressure remains over the local area today, beginning to shift \noffshore with the overhead ridge. Another day of sunny skies and dry \nweather is in store with highs reaching above average in the mid to \nupper 60s for most (slightly cooler along the immediate coast). Lows \ntonight look mostly above freezing, with only locally cooler \nlocations reaching freezing. Temperatures Friday and Saturday will \nbe similar with highs in the mid to upper 60s and lows in the 30s \nand 40s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Keeping a close eye on coastal low development \nthis weekend. \n\nAs an upper trough over the Gulf of America moves east, an area of \nlow pressure will develop along a stalled frontal boundary over the \nnorthern Bahamas later Friday or Friday night, possibly\ndeveloping subtropical characteristics. After this, the models \nare very uncertain regarding the eventual development, track and\nspeed of the low itself. Models continue to show \ninconsistencies with the 00z/19 GEFS continuing to show the \ntrack further east, stronger, and more progressive than the \n00z/19 EPS and GEPS. Despite the EPS and GEPS showing a similar \ntrack, there is large differences in the total amount of \nrainfall with the EPS showing an average of 0.75-1.75\" of \nrainfall and the GEPS showing 0.25-0.75\" of rainfall. The \neventual track of the low will be critical if any impacts occur \nover the area. Obviously the closer to the coast (or if it moves\ninland over NC), beneficial rain would spread across much of \nthe area. Meanwhile, the further offshore track would confine \nany precip to areas immediately at the coast or even mostly \noffshore. Additionally, there may be some gusty SE- NE winds at \nthe coast. Despite the uncertainties, the best rainfall chances \nwill be in areas east of I-95, not relieving drought conditions \nin the west.",
